  "text": "A statistical description of the balance between helical and random coil sections in protein‑like chains is examined. By applying a technique originally developed for magnetic spin systems, an exact expression for the overall statistical weight of all possible configurations is obtained. When the chain is very long, this expression simplifies to easy‑to‑use formulas that capture the behavior near the point where the helix begins to appear. Two situations are considered: one where the smallest uninterrupted random segment can be a single unit, and another where it must contain three units, the latter being relevant for the common type of helical structure in proteins. The equilibrium is visualized as a surface showing how many molecules occupy each state, and the size of fluctuations in the amount of helical bonding is shown to control how abrupt the transition looks. Experimental measurements on peptide samples are fitted to the theory, providing estimates of the heat and entropy changes associated with forming one helical unit."
